`,s.jsx(N,{items:[{q:"Why does a bidirectional LSTM help NER but not language modelling?",a:"NER labels a token using its full context, and the evidence often follows — 'Washington' is a person or a place depending on the next word, so reading right-to-left as well genuinely helps. Language modelling predicts the next token, so a backward pass would let the model see the answer it is being asked for. Bidirectionality is legitimate only when the entire sequence is available and is not itself the target."},{q:"What is a language model actually trained to do, and why is that so useful?",a:"Predict the next token given all previous ones — a self-supervised objective needing no labels, so any text corpus is training data. That is why it scales: the pretraining signal is unlimited, and the representations learned from it transfer to nearly every downstream language task."}]}),`
